Aim

To write a C program to design and simulate a Non-Deterministic Finite Automaton (NFA) that accepts strings over the alphabet {0, 1} which start with '0' and end with '1', using the subset (set-of-states) simulation method.

NFA Design
	
States	q0, q1, q2
Alphabet (Σ)	{0, 1}
Start state	q0
Accepting state	q2

Transition Table (non-deterministic — note the multi-valued cell)

State	on 0	on 1
→q0	{q1}	{}
q1	{q1}	{q1, q2}
*q2	{}	{}

The nondeterminism is at q1 on input 1: the NFA guesses whether that 1 is the final symbol (moves to q2, accepting) or just another symbol in the middle (stays in q1).

Algorithm
Start with the current state set = {q0}.
Read the input one symbol at a time.
For each symbol, compute the next state set = union of nfa[state][symbol] over every state currently in the set.
Repeat until the string is fully consumed.
If the final state set contains q2, ACCEPT; otherwise REJECT.
(If the state set ever becomes empty, it stays empty — equivalent to a dead state.)
